---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import { formatCards, productionUrl, resourceLinks, sharingTargets } from '../data/site';
---

<MainLayout
  title="XML.Style: Make your XML files look better with an XSLT stylesheet"
  h1="XML Styling with XSLT"
  path="/"
  shareTargets={sharingTargets}
  productionUrl={productionUrl}
>
  <h2>What is this?</h2>

  <p>
    Most websites have several <code>.xml</code> files that are used by search engines and other
    services to index and follow the site.
  </p>

  <p>
    By default, these files look horrible when a human looks at them in a browser. But there is a
    simple way to make them look better: add an XSLT line to your XML file!
  </p>

  <div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-3">
    {
      formatCards.map((format) => (
        <div class="col">
          <div class="card m-3" style="width: 20rem;">
            <div class="card-body p-0">
              <h3 class="card-title p-3 border-bottom my-0">
                <img src={format.icon} alt={format.title} class="pe-2" style="height: 1.5em;" />
                {format.title}
              </h3>
              <div class="card-text px-3 py-2" set:html={format.textHtml} />
              <div class="card-footer d-flex justify-content-between">
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm" href={format.url}>
                  Instructions
                </a>
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm" href={format.demo}>
                  {format.demoText ?? 'Demo'}
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="how">How do I use it?</h2>

  <p>
    You need to have a XSLT stylesheet along with your <code>.xml</code> file. Download one of the
    ones above or created your own with the help of the <a href="#links">resources linked below</a
    >.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the top of your XML file, it will look like:
  </p>
  <pre class="m-4 p-3 bg-light border rounded">
&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;data-depends-on-type&gt;
...
  </pre>

  <p>
    Add a line in the middle so it looks like:
  </p>
  <pre class="m-4 p-3 bg-light border rounded">
&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="my-downloaded-file.xslt"?&gt;
&lt;data-depends-on-type&gt;
...
  </pre>

  <h2 id="warn">Gotchas</h2>

  <p>A couple key points that I learned the hard way:</p>

  <ul>
    <li class="py-1">You must serve the page with the content type <code>text/xml</code>.</li>
    <li class="py-1">The stylesheet must be on the same host as the XML file.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Otherwise various browsers will ignore the stylesheet. See the
    <a href="https://www.rss.style/content-type.html">test page</a> for more details.
  </p>

  <h2 id="links">Resources</h2>
  <ul class="pb-3">
    {
      resourceLinks.map((link) => (
        <li class="py-1">
          <a href={link.url}>{link.title}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</MainLayout>
